  "sounds": [
   {
    "title": "Even beats, not stress",
    "body": "English leans hard on one syllable of a word. Japanese gives every kana the same length instead — one beat each. (Small kana — ゃ/ゅ/ょ, plus ァ/ィ/ゥ/ェ/ォ in loanwords — fuse with the kana before them into a single beat: きょう and ファン are two beats each.) kamera is ka-me-ra, three equal beats, never ka-MEH-ra. Flattening out the stress is most of the accent work.",
    "sample": {
     "text": "カメラ",
     "reading": "ka-me-ra",
     "meaning": "camera — three equal beats"
    }
   },
   {
    "title": "Long and short are different words",
    "body": "obasan is an aunt; obāsan is a grandmother. The small っ counts as a held beat of silence, so kite means 'come' and kitte means 'postage stamp'. Length is not decoration here — it is what tells the two apart.",
    "sample": {
     "text": "きて / きって",
     "reading": "kite / kitte",
     "meaning": "come / postage stamp"
    }
   },
   {
    "title": "The Japanese r is a single quick tap",
    "body": "It is neither English r nor l. The tip of the tongue flicks once against the ridge behind the top teeth, close to the tt in the American pronunciation of 'butter'. No rolling, no full l — just one light tap.",
    "sample": {
     "text": "さくら",
     "reading": "sakura",
     "meaning": "cherry blossom"
    }
   }
  ],
  "order": {
   "type": "Subject → Object → Verb",
   "title": "The verb waits until the very end",
